  "customfield_10187": null,
  "description": "<p>Fedora should not allow the use of `ldp:contains` for the `ldp:hasMemberRelation` Object on Direct and Indirect containers.</p>\n\n<p>In the example of Direct Containers, the following scenario should be prohibited:</p>\n<ol>\n\t<li>Create a resource<br/>\n&gt; curl -i -u fedoraAdmin:fedoraAdmin -XPUT <a href=\"http://localhost:8080/rest/dresource\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8080/rest/dresource</a></li>\n</ol>\n\n\n<ol>\n\t<li>Create a Direct Container:<br/>\n&gt; curl -i -u fedoraAdmin:fedoraAdmin -XPUT <a href=\"http://localhost:8080/rest/d\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8080/rest/d</a> -H \"Link: &lt;<a href=\"http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#DirectContainer\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#DirectContainer</a>&gt;;rel=\\\"type\\\"\"</li>\n</ol>\n\n\n<ol>\n\t<li>Add Direct Container triples to the new container:<br/>\n&gt; curl -i -u fedoraAdmin:fedoraAdmin -XPATCH <a href=\"http://localhost:8080/rest/d\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">http://localhost:8080/rest/d</a> -H \"Content-Type: application/sparql-update\" --data-binary @direct.su<br/>\n...where direct.su is:<br/>\n```<br/>\nINSERT \n{\r\n&lt;&gt; &lt;http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#membershipResource&gt; &lt;/rest/dresource&gt; ;\r\n&lt;http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#hasMemberRelation&gt; &lt;http://www.w3.org/ns/ldp#contains&gt; .\r\n}\n<p> WHERE {}<br/>\n```<br/>\nThe above PUT should fail because \"ldp:hasMemberRelation\" is set to \"ldp:contains\"</p></li>\n</ol>\n\n\n<p>Currently, in the above scenario, any resource that is added to &lt;/rest/d&gt; will result in a triple on &lt;/rest/dresource&gt; of the form:<br/>\n&lt;/rest/dresource&gt; ldp:contains &lt;child-of-d&gt;</p>\n\n<p>...which is bad</p>\n\n<p>Note 1: The above scenario should also be prevented for Indirect Containers.<br/>\nNote 2: If using PUT in the above scenario with the Direct Container triples, the PUT fails as expected.</p>",
